      "title": "To John Higgins 27 May [1846]1",
      "header_text": [
        "To John Higgins 27 May [1846]1Down Farnborough KentMay 27th"
      ],
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "body_paragraph": 1,
          "text": "I write to acknowledge the draft for 190£ 13s 1d & to thank you for it.—",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 1"
        },
        {
          "body_paragraph": 2,
          "text": "I am very glad Mr. Hardy is going on well.",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 2"
        },
        {
          "body_paragraph": 3,
          "text": "Will you be so good as to give me as long a notice as you conveniently can, when you want the money for the new Farm House, as my Father owing to some arrangements has been rather short of money this Spring, but I apprehend, very soon now all times will be equally convenient to him.—",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 3"
        },
        {
          "body_paragraph": 4,
          "text": "My Bankers are Mrs Robarts, Curtis & Co & I shd be glad, if, as you propose, you would pay the rent direct into their hands for the future, being so good as to notify me of the same.—",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 4"
        },
        {
          "body_paragraph": 5,
          "text": "I wish I could have the pleasure of seeing you this week, but I shall not be up in London.",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 5"
        },
        {
          "body_paragraph": 6,
          "text": "Dear Sir | Yours very faithfully | C. Darwin",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 6"
        },
        {
          "body_paragraph": 7,
          "text": "P.S. | Do not forget the steeper pitch of roof in the new farm House & oak Lintels.",
          "html_class": "",
          "locator": "div#letter div.body-content; retained body block 7"
        }
      ],
      "excluded_body_annotations_and_salutations": [
        {
          "class": "salute",
          "text": "Dear Sir"
        }
      ],
      "body_container_count": 1,
      "used_whole_body_fallback": false,
      "editorial_summary": "Summary Acknowledges receipt of draft. When does JH want the money for the new farmhouse? Bankers are Robarts, Curtis & Co. JH to pay them the rent directly.",
      "editorial_footnotes": "Footnotes1The date is based on an entry of that date in CD’s Account Book (Down House MS).2The half-yearly rent for Beesby Farm, less Higgins’ percentage as agent (CD’s Investment Book (Down House MS), p. 20). For the purchase of the farm, see letters from John Higgins, 15 March 1845 and 2 October 1845.3Ralph Hardy, contractor, who pulled down the old farm buildings at Beesby (R. W. Darwin’s Investment Book (Down House MS), p. 74).4CD had already borrowed £12,577 4s. from his father to purchase the farm (CD’s Investment Book, p. 19). Robert Waring Darwin advanced an additional £1,026 0s. 2d. to cover the cost of farm buildings. See CD’s Investment Book, p. 19, and R. W. Darwin’s Investment Book, p. 74 (Down House MSS).",
      "letter_container_present": true
